Feature: Automate migration of eligible files from Purgo S3 landing folder to archive folder using Databricks PySpark

  Background:
    Given Databricks secrets "access_key" and "secret_key" are available under the scope "aws_keys"
    And the Unity Catalog is "purgo_databricks" and schema is "purgo_playground"
    And the source of truth for file migration is the table "purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log"
    And the script has access to AWS S3 using the credentials from Databricks secrets

  # Happy Path: Move eligible files with file_status = 'SUCCESS'
  Scenario Outline: Move eligible files from landing to archive folder
    Given the log table "purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log" contains a file entry with
      | file_name         | <file_name>         |
      | s3_landing_path   | <s3_landing_path>   |
      | s3_archive_path   | <s3_archive_path>   |
      | file_status       | SUCCESS             |
    And the file "<file_name>" exists in S3 landing path "<s3_landing_path>"
    When the migration script is executed
    Then the file "<file_name>" is moved from "<s3_landing_path>" to "<s3_archive_path>"
    And the file "<file_name>" no longer exists in "<s3_landing_path>"
    And the file "<file_name>" exists in "<s3_archive_path>"
    And the folder structure is preserved in the archive location
    And no error is logged

    Examples:
      | file_name           | s3_landing_path                                                      | s3_archive_path                                                      |
      | patient_20240601.csv| s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw            | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive        |
      | visit_20240601.csv  | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/visit_raw              | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/visit_archive          |
      | lab_20240601.csv    | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/lab_raw                | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/lab_archive            |

  # Error Path: File status is not 'SUCCESS'
  Scenario Outline: Do not move files with non-SUCCESS status
    Given the log table "purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log" contains a file entry with
      | file_name         | <file_name>         |
      | s3_landing_path   | <s3_landing_path>   |
      | s3_archive_path   | <s3_archive_path>   |
      | file_status       | <file_status>       |
    And the file "<file_name>" exists in S3 landing path "<s3_landing_path>"
    When the migration script is executed
    Then the file "<file_name>" is not moved to "<s3_archive_path>"
    And the file "<file_name>" remains in "<s3_landing_path>"
    And an info message "File status is not SUCCESS. Skipping file: <file_name>" is logged

    Examples:
      | file_name           | s3_landing_path                                                      | s3_archive_path                                                      | file_status |
      | patient_20240602.csv| s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw            | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive        | FAILED      |
      | visit_20240602.csv  | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/visit_raw              | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/visit_archive          | IN_PROGRESS |
      | lab_20240602.csv    | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/lab_raw                | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/lab_archive            | ERROR       |

  # Error Path: File does not exist in landing path
  Scenario Outline: Handle missing file in landing path
    Given the log table "purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log" contains a file entry with
      | file_name         | <file_name>         |
      | s3_landing_path   | <s3_landing_path>   |
      | s3_archive_path   | <s3_archive_path>   |
      | file_status       | SUCCESS             |
    And the file "<file_name>" does not exist in S3 landing path "<s3_landing_path>"
    When the migration script is executed
    Then the file "<file_name>" is not moved to "<s3_archive_path>"
    And an error message "File not found in landing path: <s3_landing_path>/<file_name>" is logged

    Examples:
      | file_name           | s3_landing_path                                                      | s3_archive_path                                                      |
      | patient_20240603.csv| s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw            | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive        |
      | visit_20240603.csv  | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/visit_raw              | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/visit_archive          |

  # Error Path: Archive path does not exist
  Scenario Outline: Create archive path if missing
    Given the log table "purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log" contains a file entry with
      | file_name         | <file_name>         |
      | s3_landing_path   | <s3_landing_path>   |
      | s3_archive_path   | <s3_archive_path>   |
      | file_status       | SUCCESS             |
    And the file "<file_name>" exists in S3 landing path "<s3_landing_path>"
    And the archive path "<s3_archive_path>" does not exist in S3
    When the migration script is executed
    Then the archive path "<s3_archive_path>" is created in S3
    And the file "<file_name>" is moved from "<s3_landing_path>" to "<s3_archive_path>"
    And no error is logged

    Examples:
      | file_name           | s3_landing_path                                                      | s3_archive_path                                                      |
      | patient_20240604.csv| s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw            | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive_new    |

  # Error Path: AWS credentials missing or invalid
  Scenario: Handle missing AWS credentials
    Given Databricks secret "access_key" is missing under scope "aws_keys"
    Or Databricks secret "secret_key" is missing under scope "aws_keys"
    When the migration script is executed
    Then the script fails to connect to S3
    And an error message "AWS credentials not found in Databricks secret scope: aws_keys" is logged

  # Error Path: S3 permission denied
  Scenario Outline: Handle S3 permission denied error
    Given the log table "purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log" contains a file entry with
      | file_name         | <file_name>         |
      | s3_landing_path   | <s3_landing_path>   |
      | s3_archive_path   | <s3_archive_path>   |
      | file_status       | SUCCESS             |
    And the file "<file_name>" exists in S3 landing path "<s3_landing_path>"
    And the AWS credentials do not have permission to move files from "<s3_landing_path>" to "<s3_archive_path>"
    When the migration script is executed
    Then the file "<file_name>" is not moved to "<s3_archive_path>"
    And an error message "Permission denied for S3 operation on: <s3_landing_path>/<file_name>" is logged

    Examples:
      | file_name           | s3_landing_path                                                      | s3_archive_path                                                      |
      | patient_20240605.csv| s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw            | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive        |

  # Validation: S3 path format
  Scenario Outline: Validate S3 path format in log table
    Given the log table "purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log" contains a file entry with
      | file_name         | <file_name>         |
      | s3_landing_path   | <s3_landing_path>   |
      | s3_archive_path   | <s3_archive_path>   |
      | file_status       | SUCCESS             |
    When the migration script is executed
    Then the S3 path "<s3_landing_path>" must match the format "s3://<bucket>/<folder>/<subfolder>"
    And the S3 path "<s3_archive_path>" must match the format "s3://<bucket>/<folder>/<subfolder>"
    And if the path format is invalid, an error message "Invalid S3 path format in log table for file: <file_name>" is logged

    Examples:
      | file_name           | s3_landing_path                                                      | s3_archive_path                                                      |
      | patient_20240606.csv| s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw            | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive        |
      | badfile.csv         | agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw                 | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive        |

  # Validation: Duplicate file entries
  Scenario Outline: Handle duplicate file entries in log table
    Given the log table "purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log" contains multiple entries for file "<file_name>" with
      | file_status       | <file_status_1>     |
      | file_status       | <file_status_2>     |
    And the file "<file_name>" exists in S3 landing path "<s3_landing_path>"
    When the migration script is executed
    Then only the entry with "file_status = SUCCESS" is considered for migration
    And the file "<file_name>" is moved to "<s3_archive_path>" if eligible
    And an info message "Multiple log entries found for file: <file_name>. Only SUCCESS status processed." is logged

    Examples:
      | file_name           | s3_landing_path                                                      | s3_archive_path                                                      | file_status_1 | file_status_2 |
      | patient_20240607.csv| s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw            | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive        | SUCCESS       | FAILED        |
      | visit_20240607.csv  | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/visit_raw              | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/visit_archive          | IN_PROGRESS   | SUCCESS       |

  # Validation: File types and formats
  Scenario Outline: Validate file type and format before migration
    Given the log table "purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log" contains a file entry with
      | file_name         | <file_name>         |
      | s3_landing_path   | <s3_landing_path>   |
      | s3_archive_path   | <s3_archive_path>   |
      | file_status       | SUCCESS             |
    And the file "<file_name>" exists in S3 landing path "<s3_landing_path>"
    When the migration script is executed
    Then the file "<file_name>" must be of type CSV, TXT, or PARQUET
    And if the file type is unsupported, an error message "Unsupported file type for file: <file_name>" is logged

    Examples:
      | file_name           | s3_landing_path                                                      | s3_archive_path                                                      |
      | patient_20240608.csv| s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw            | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive        |
      | visit_20240608.txt  | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/visit_raw              | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/visit_archive          |
      | lab_20240608.parquet| s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/lab_raw                | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/lab_archive            |
      | image_20240608.jpg  | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/image_raw              | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/image_archive          |

  # Validation: Audit logging
  Scenario Outline: Audit migration status in log table
    Given the log table "purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log" contains a file entry with
      | file_name         | <file_name>         |
      | s3_landing_path   | <s3_landing_path>   |
      | s3_archive_path   | <s3_archive_path>   |
      | file_status       | SUCCESS             |
    And the file "<file_name>" exists in S3 landing path "<s3_landing_path>"
    When the migration script is executed
    Then the migration status for "<file_name>" is updated in the log table to "ARCHIVED"
    And the file_processed_date is updated to the current timestamp

    Examples:
      | file_name           | s3_landing_path                                                      | s3_archive_path                                                      |
      | patient_20240609.csv| s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw            | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive        |
      | visit_20240609.csv  | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/visit_raw              | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/visit_archive          |

  # Validation: Migration scope by date
  Scenario Outline: Migrate only files processed within a specific date range
    Given the log table "purgo_playground.s3_file_process_log" contains a file entry with
      | file_name           | <file_name>         |
      | s3_landing_path     | <s3_landing_path>   |
      | s3_archive_path     | <s3_archive_path>   |
      | file_status         | SUCCESS             |
      | file_processed_date | <file_processed_date>|
    And the file "<file_name>" exists in S3 landing path "<s3_landing_path>"
    When the migration script is executed for date range "<start_date>" to "<end_date>"
    Then only files with file_processed_date between "<start_date>" and "<end_date>" are migrated

    Examples:
      | file_name           | s3_landing_path                                                      | s3_archive_path                                                      | file_processed_date    | start_date   | end_date     |
      | patient_20240610.csv| s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_raw            | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/patient_archive        | 2024-06-10T10:00:00Z   | 2024-06-10   | 2024-06-10   |
      | visit_20240611.csv  | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/visit_raw              | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/visit_archive          | 2024-06-11T11:00:00Z   | 2024-06-11   | 2024-06-11   |
      | lab_20240612.csv    | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/lab_raw                | s3://agilisium-playground-dev/filestore/purgo/lab_archive            | 2024-06-12T12:00:00Z   | 2024-06-10   | 2024-06-11   |
